  "chapter": "Chapter 3. Insurance and Indemnification",
  "section": "3.6. Workers' Compensation Insurance",
  "subsection": "F. Workers' Compensation Insurance",
  "text": "4. [3:545] Coverage for Penalties Imposed by WCAB: In certain cases, additional sums may be recoverable against a workers' compensation insurance carrier: a. [3:546] Penalty for “unreasonable delay or refusal to pay benefits”: The WCAB may impose a penalty of 25% of the total award, or $10,000, whichever is less, where compensation benefits have been “unreasonably delayed or refused.” [Lab.C. § 5814(a) (emphasis added)] (The 25% penalty does not apply where the employer discovers a potential violation before the employee claims the 25% penalty and, within 90 days after discovery, pays the benefits due plus a self-imposed penalty of 10%; see Lab.C. § 5814(b).) 3 (1) [3:547] “Unreasonably”: “Unreasonably” is tested objectively. The only satisfactory excuse for a delay or refusal is genuine doubt, from a medical or legal standpoint, as to the employer's liability for benefits. The carrier bears the burden of proving such genuine doubt. [Ralph's Grocery Co. v. WCAB (1995) 38 CA4th 820, 830, 45 CR2d 197, 203] [3:547.1 - 3:547.4] Reserved.",
